{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779256099,"nonce":"pZEpY5Q9YiDHuCsiidvFOwmFz84=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABCz1oyQ","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/144","origin":"github"},"title":"marine/response bidirectional bridging creates feedback loop (latent since 2026-03-27)","message":"## Problem\n\n`marine/response` is bridged **bidirectionally** by `udp_bridge` between boat and operator in `bizzyboat_project11/config/bizzyboat.yaml`:\n\n```yaml\n# Boat-side bridge\ntopics:\n  command_response: {source: marine/response}\n\n# Operator-side bridge\ntopics:\n  command_response: {source: marine/response}\n```\n\nBoth sides subscribe to their local `marine/response` and forward any received `command_response` to their own local `marine/response`. That creates a feedback loop:\n\n1. Boat publishes `marine/response` (an ack from `command_bridge_receiver`)\n2. Boat bridge subscribes, forwards to operator as `command_response`\n3. Operator bridge receives, publishes to operator-local `marine/response`\n4. Operator bridge ALSO subscribes to local `marine/response` (its own publication), forwards back as `command_response`\n5. Boat bridge receives, publishes to boat-local `marine/response`, sees its own re-publication, forwards back\n6. Loop runs at bridge-sustained rate\n\n## Evidence (2026-05-19 deployment, [#135](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/135))\n\nDuring the 13:12–13:29 EDT failure window, raw bag replay of `/bizzy/udp_bridge/topic_statistics` shows `/bizzy/marine/response` at **48,990 msg/s** (vs ~0 baseline). Across 3 contributing buckets (init + vpn-destination + wifi-destination), that single topic accounts for ~150k of the ~132k summed `messages_per_second` spike. See [PR #136](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/pull/136) dev log post-mission §4 corrections + investigation chain.\n\nBag SQLite recording for `/bizzy/marine/response` shows only 0/s — that's a recording artifact (QoS-depth-10 subscription can't keep up with 48k/s), not the topic's true rate.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- **Generates ~150k UDP packets/s of bridge traffic** when the loop runs. Many lost on lossy WiFi → resend cycle → \"Giving up on resend\" WARN spam at thousands/sec (separately tracked in [`rolker/udp_bridge`](https://github.com/rolker/udp_bridge) issues).\n- **Latent since commit `9985192` (2026-03-27)** — the original add of `bizzyboat_project11`. The bidirectional config has been there from day one. Most deployments haven't blown up because the loop is normally throttled by something operational; today's nav-restart at 13:13 broke whatever was preventing it.\n- **Operator-side stats panels were already frozen** ([`rolker/udp_bridge#20`](https://github.com/rolker/udp_bridge/issues/20)) during the storm, so the operator had no visibility into the bridge's actual workload.\n\n## Why the loop activates sometimes but not always\n\nNot fully pinned. Hypotheses:\n- Under normal ops, the loop runs once per actual `marine/response` ack from `command_bridge_receiver` (1 ack per real command, typically a few per minute). The bridge round-trip is fast but bounded by ack frequency.\n- Something at today's nav-restart at 13:13 broke whatever was keeping the bridge from re-subscribing to its own publication, OR caused command_bridge_receiver to publish at rate-unlimited speed (e.g., the receiver getting flooded with commands).\n- Once the loop is in sustained mode, it self-sustains at whatever the bridge can throughput-handle (~48k/s today).\n\nWorth investigating further if/when reproducible.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nThree shapes:\n\n1. **Make `marine/response` unidirectional in bridge config.** Boat publishes acks; operator should not be sending acks for anything. Operator-side bridge shouldn't subscribe to `marine/response` at all. Single config change.\n2. **Add bridge-level message-origin tracking** — bridge tags messages it forwards so it won't pick up its own re-publication. More invasive change in `rolker/udp_bridge`.\n3. **command_bridge_receiver dedup** already exists by timestamp, but the bridge feedback bypasses it because each loop iteration creates a \"new\" message ROS-publish-wise (different ROS message sequence number) even if the String content is identical.\n\nOption 1 is the smallest and most aligned with the actual data flow — operator never originates marine/response, so unidirectional makes sense.\n\n## Audit suggestion\n\nOther bridged topics in `bizzyboat.yaml` should be audited for similar bidirectional-when-it-shouldn't-be patterns:\n\n- `command_response` (this issue)\n- Any other topic where only ONE side originates messages but BOTH sides bridge\n\nQuick audit: which entries appear in both boat-side and operator-side `topics:` blocks should be intentional (e.g., truly bidirectional topics like `cmd_vel` if applicable) vs accidental.\n\n## Related\n\n- [PR #136 / 2026-05-19 deployment](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/135) — discovery context.\n- [`rolker/udp_bridge#20`](https://github.com/rolker/udp_bridge/issues/20) — operator-side stats publication stall, independent issue.\n- Sibling issue (to be filed) on `rolker/udp_bridge` about the \"Giving up on resend\" WARN log spam introduced by `udp_bridge#13`.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}